<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<worksheet>
  <title>2.7. General Fourier series</title>

  <p><alert>MTH 401</alert></p>

  <definition xml:id="def-general-fourier-series">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>f(x)</m> be a function on the interval <m>[-L,L]</m>. Then the 
        <term>fourier series</term> for <m>f</m> on <m>[-L,L]</m> is 
        <md>
         \ds f(x) = A_0 + \sum\limits_{n=1}^{\infty} \left[ A_n \cos\left(\frac{n \pi x}{L}\right)
         + B_n \sin\left(\frac{n \pi x}{L}\right)\right]
        </md>
        with coefficients <m>A_0</m> and <m>A_n, B_n</m> for <m>n=1, 2, \ldots</m> given by
        <md>
          <mrow>A_0 \amp = \frac{1}{2L}\int_{-L}^{L} f(x) ~dx </mrow>
          <mrow>A_n \amp = \frac{1}{L}\int_{-L}^{L} f(x)\cos\left(\frac{n\pi x}{L}\right) ~dx </mrow>
          <mrow>B_n \amp = \frac{1}{L}\int_{-L}^{L} f(x)\sin\left(\frac{n\pi x}{L}\right) ~dx </mrow>
        </md>
        
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <definition xml:id="def-odd-even-functions">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Recall <m>f(x)</m> is an <term>even function</term> if <m>f(x) = f(-x)</m>, and 
        <m>f(x)</m> is an <term>odd function</term> if <m>{f(x)=-f(-x)}</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <exercise>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        First recall the Fourier sine and cosine series on <m>[0,L]</m> (not than the general Fourier series yet).
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="3in">
      <statement>
        <p>Find the Fourier sine series for the function <m>f(x) = x</m> over
  the interval <m>[0,1]</m>.  Plot an approximation to your series
  along with the function <m>f(x)</m> over this interval.</p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="S2-7-soln">
        <p>
          The Fourier Sine Series becomes <m>{x = \sum_{n=1}^{\infty} A_n \sin\left(\frac{n\pi x}{1}\right)}</m>
          which has solution
          <md>
            A_n = \frac21 \int_0^1 x\sin(n\pi x)~dx = \frac{-2(-1)^n}{n\pi}.
          </md>
          So the Fourier Sine series is 
          <md>
            x = \sum_{n=1}^{\infty} \frac{-2(-1)^n}{n\pi}\sin(n\pi x).
          </md>
          Which looks like this:
        </p>
        <image width="35%">
      <shortdescription>
        The Gibb's phenomenon occurs near the x=1 boundary for the Fourier Sine series on the interval [0,1].
      </shortdescription>
      <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-2-7-fourier-sine-x">
        <diagram dimensions="(200,200)" margins="25">
          <definition>f(x)=-2*(-1)^1/(1*pi)*sin(1*x*pi)-2*(-1)^2/(2*pi)*sin(2*x*pi)-2*(-1)^3/(3*pi)*sin(3*pi*x)
          </definition>
          <definition>g(x)=-2*(-1)^4/(4*pi)*sin(4*pi*x)-2*(-1)^5/(5*pi)*sin(5*pi*x)-2*(-1)^6/(6*pi)*sin(6*pi*x)
          </definition>
          <definition>k(x)=-2*(-1)^7/(7*pi)*sin(7*pi*x)-2*(-1)^8/(8*pi)*sin(8*pi*x)-2*(-1)^9/(9*pi)*sin(9*pi*x)
          </definition>
          <definition>L(x)=-2*(-1)^(10)/(10*pi)*sin(10*pi*x)-2*(-1)^(11)/(11*pi)*sin(11*pi*x)-2*(-1)^(12)/(12*pi)*sin(12*pi*x)
          </definition>
          <definition>P(x)=-2*(-1)^(13)/(13*pi)*sin(13*pi*x)-2*(-1)^(14)/(14*pi)*sin(14*pi*x)-2*(-1)^(15)/(15*pi)*sin(15*pi*x)
          </definition>
          <definition>h(x)=f(x)+g(x)+k(x)+L(x)+P(x)</definition>
          
          <coordinates bbox="(0,0,1,1)">
            <grid-axes decorations="yes" spacings="((0,1,1),(0,1,1))"
            hlabels="(0,1,1)" vlabels="(0,1,1)"/>
            <graph function="h" />
           

          </coordinates>
        </diagram>
      </prefigure>
    </image>
    <p>
      I plotted only the first 15 terms in the sum because I got tired.  On the right boundary at <m>x=1</m> we see this oscillation, called the Gibb's phenomenon.  Interesting.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="2.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>Now find the Fourier cosine series for this function <m>f(x) = x</m> over the same
  interval <m>[0,1]</m>.  Plot an approximation to your series
  along with the function <m>f(x)</m> over this interval.</p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="S2-7-soln">
        <p>
Set up is <m>{x = A_0 + \sum_{n=1}^{\infty} A_n\cos(n\pi x)}</m> and has solutions
<m>{A_0 = \frac11\int_0^1 x~dx = \frac12}</m> and 
<m>{A_n = \frac21\int_0^1 x\cos(n\pi x)~dx = \frac{2(-1+(-1)^n)}{n^2\pi^2}}</m>. Thus the series becomes
<md>\frac12 + \sum_{n=1}^{\infty}  \frac{2(-1+(-1)^n)}{n^2\pi^2} \cos(n\pi x)</md>. This looks like the plot below - no Gibb's phenomenon.  Very interesting.
    </p>
    <image width="35%">
      <shortdescription>
        The Gibb's phenomenon does not occur for the Fourier Cosine series on the interval [0,1].
      </shortdescription>
      <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-2-7-fourier-cosine-x">
        <diagram dimensions="(200,200)" margins="25">
       
          <definition>q(x,k)=2*(-1+(-1)^k)/(k^2*pi^2)*cos(k*x*pi)</definition>
          <definition>p(x)=1/2 + q(x,1)+q(x,2)+q(x,3)+q(x,4)+q(x,5)+q(x,6)+q(x,7)+q(x,8)+q(x,9)+q(x,10)+q(x,11)+q(x,12)+q(x,13)+q(x,14)+q(x,15)+q(x,16)+q(x,17)

          </definition>
          
          
          <coordinates bbox="(0,0,1,1)">
            <grid-axes decorations="yes" spacings="((0,1,1),(0,1,1))"
            hlabels="(0,1,1)" vlabels="(0,1,1)"/>
           
            <graph function="p" />
           

          </coordinates>
        </diagram>
      </prefigure>
    </image>
      </solution>
    </task>
  </exercise>

   <p>You should see the <term>Gibbs phenomenon</term>, an oscillating behavior at an endpoint,
     in one case but not the other.
  We'd like to understand why this happens.  </p>

  <exercise>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Let's look at another example.
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="2.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>Find the <em>general Fourier series</em> for the function <m>f(x) = x^2 + x
  + 1</m> over the interval <m>[-1,1]</m>.</p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="S2-7-soln">
        <p>
          The setup:
          <md>
            f(x) = x^2+x+1 = A_0+\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}A_n\cos(n\pi x)+B_n\sin(n\pi x).
          </md>
          Coefficients are then:
          <md>
            <mrow>A_0 \amp=\frac12\int_{-1}^1 (x^2+x+1)~dx = \frac43 </mrow>
            <mrow>A_n \amp=\frac11\int_{-1}^1 (x^2+x+1)\cos(n\pi x)~dx = \frac{4(-1)^n}{n^2\pi^2} </mrow>
            <mrow>B_n \amp=\frac11\int_{-1}^1 (x^2+x+1)\sin(n\pi x)~dx = \frac{-2(-1)^n}{n\pi} </mrow>
          </md>
          And all together now:
          <md>
            x^2+x+1 = \frac43+\sum_{n=1}^{\infty} \frac{4(-1)^n}{n^2\pi^2}\cos(n\pi x)+\frac{-2(-1)^n}{n\pi}\sin(n\pi x).
          </md>
          
          
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>Use Mathematica (or your choice of technology) to graph the function <m>f(x)</m> and an approximation to
  the Fourier series over the interval <m>[-1,1]</m>.  No matter how many
  terms you add in the series approximation, your Fourier series should exhibit the <em>Gibbs
  phenomenon</em>, which shows a lot of oscillations near the endpoints.</p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="S2-7-soln">
        <image width="35%">
      <shortdescription>
        The Gibb's phenomenon does occur at both x=-1 and x=1 for the Fourier series of x^2+x+1 on the interval [-1,1].
      </shortdescription>
      <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-2-7-gibbs">
        <diagram dimensions="(200,200)" margins="25">
       
          <definition>q(x,k)=(4*(-1)^k)/(k^2*pi^2)*cos(k*x*pi)-2*(-1)^k/(k*pi)*sin(k*pi*x)</definition>
          <definition>p(x)=4/3 + q(x,1)+q(x,2)+q(x,3)+q(x,4)+q(x,5)+q(x,6)+q(x,7)+q(x,8)+q(x,9)+q(x,10)+q(x,11)+q(x,12)+q(x,13)+q(x,14)+q(x,15)+q(x,16)+q(x,17)+q(x,18)+q(x,19)+q(x,20)+q(x,21)+q(x,22)+q(x,23)+q(x,24)

          </definition>
          
          
          <coordinates bbox="(-1,-1,1,3.2)">
            <grid-axes decorations="yes" spacings="((0,1,1),(0,2,1))"
            hlabels="(0,1,1)" vlabels="(0,2,1)"/>
           
            <graph function="p" />
           

          </coordinates>
        </diagram>
      </prefigure>
    </image>
    <p>
      I didn't use Mathematica here but it's the same idea, and I took it to <m>n=24</m> before I got tired...
    </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>Now graph just the Fourier series over the interval <m>[-4,4]</m>.  Here
  you should see the periodicity in the Fourier series and the reason
  for the Gibbs phenomenon as the Fourier series is trying to track
  a discontinuity.</p>
        
      </statement>
      <solution component="S2-7-soln">
        <p>
          Still the sum of the first 24 terms.
        </p>
        <image width="35%">
      <shortdescription>
        The Gibb's phenomenon does occur for the Fourier series of x^2+x+1 on the interval [-1,1], extended to see the extension past [-1,1].
      </shortdescription>
      <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-2-7-fourier-cosine-x2">
        <diagram dimensions="(200,200)" margins="25">
       
          <definition>q(x,k)=(4*(-1)^k)/(k^2*pi^2)*cos(k*x*pi)-2*(-1)^k/(k*pi)*sin(k*pi*x)</definition>
          <definition>p(x)=4/3 + q(x,1)+q(x,2)+q(x,3)+q(x,4)+q(x,5)+q(x,6)+q(x,7)+q(x,8)+q(x,9)+q(x,10)+q(x,11)+q(x,12)+q(x,13)+q(x,14)+q(x,15)+q(x,16)+q(x,17)+q(x,18)+q(x,19)+q(x,20)+q(x,21)+q(x,22)+q(x,23)+q(x,24)

          </definition>
          
          
          <coordinates bbox="(-4,-1,4,3.2)">
            <grid-axes decorations="yes" spacings="((-4,1,4),(0,2,1))"
            hlabels="(-4,1,4)" vlabels="(0,2,1)"/>
           
            <graph function="p" />
           

          </coordinates>
        </diagram>
      </prefigure>
    </image>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="2in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Stay tuned! We'll discuss this as a class in a little while.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

</worksheet>
